# The spend ledger is APPEND ONLY, so a merge should keep every line from both sides.
#
# CLAUDE.md states the rule already: "On a out/translatv/spend_log.jsonl merge conflict,
# KEEP BOTH SIDES. Never drop a ledger row to resolve a conflict." This makes git do it instead
# of a person remembering to at whatever hour the conflict appeared.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, because the first version of this comment claimed otherwise. `union`
# resolves a CONFLICTING HUNK by concatenating both sides. It does nothing about a change that
# does not conflict, and a deleted row usually does not: verified, base of four rows, one side
# deletes a row while the other appends far away, the merge is clean and the row is gone. So the
# CLAUDE.md rule holding any pull request that REMOVES ledger rows is still load bearing and is
# not made redundant by this file.
#
# Within a conflicting hunk, though, the direction is the safe one. Under logging is the harmful
# failure: a resolution that drops rows understates what the project has cost and hands the cap
# gate a budget it has already spent. Union can only duplicate instead, which overstates, is
# visible to `sort | uniq -d`, and is harmless by comparison. Two sides editing the header line
# also duplicates the rows between them, for the same reason.
#
# It is also LOCAL ONLY. GitHub's merge button does not run user defined merge drivers, so a pull
# request whose ledger conflicts is refused there rather than union merged, and is resolved in a
# clone. And the very first merge that brings this file in does not get it either, since the
# attribute has to be present on both sides already: that one is still resolved by hand.
out/translatv/spend_log.jsonl merge=union

# The view is GENERATED, and is deliberately NOT given an attribute here.
#
# `merge=ours` was tried and removed. It does not work: git's built in low level drivers are
# `text`, `binary` and `union` only. `ours` is a merge STRATEGY (`git merge -s ours`), which is a
# different thing, and when .gitattributes names a driver git cannot find it falls back to the
# default text merge SILENTLY. `git check-attr` happily reports `merge: ours` while the merge
# conflicts exactly as it would with no attribute at all, which is how it survived being
# "verified" once.
#
# It can be made to work with `git config merge.ours.driver true`, but that is per machine and
# cannot live in this file. A machine that has not run it gets a plain conflict and no sign the
# attribute was ignored, which is the same "someone has to remember" failure this file exists to
# remove, with a worse failure mode because it is invisible.
#
# So the .md still conflicts when two machines both regenerate it. Resolve it by regenerating
# rather than by reading the markers:
#   python3 spend_log.py render translatv --execute
# `npm run check:spend-view` fails if the committed view disagrees with the ledger, so a wrong
# resolution here is caught by CI rather than believed.
